WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:03.851
Hello and welcome. I'm James Murphy.

00:00:03.851 --> 00:00:09.750
In this video,&nbsp;we're tackling a hard interview question: The Infamous Skyline Problem.

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:11.897
I'll explain my C++&nbsp;solution

00:00:11.897 --> 00:00:18.874
which beat 98.24% of other submissions in speed and 100% in memory.

00:00:18.874 --> 00:00:21.917
In case you've never&nbsp;looked at a beautiful city view and thought:

00:00:21.917 --> 00:00:25.690
I wonder what mathematical function describes this&nbsp;skyline.

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:26.383
Don't worry.

00:00:26.383 --> 00:00:31.945
The more practical view of the problem is finding the pointwise max of a bunch&nbsp;of piecewise functions.

00:00:31.945 --> 00:00:35.871
But thinking about it like buildings is just easier to visualize.

00:00:35.871 --> 00:00:40.014
We're given&nbsp;a bunch of buildings represented by their left, right and height.

00:00:40.014 --> 00:00:43.670
And we're supposed to produce&nbsp;the skyline formed by those buildings

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:46.323
which are represented by x, y points.

00:00:46.323 --> 00:00:50.160
That description&nbsp;is a bit dense. So, let's look at it visually.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:55.120
First, we have the buildings defined by their&nbsp;left and right x coordinates and their height.

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:59.734
Their outline together with the implicit ground at&nbsp;height zero forms a skyline.

00:00:59.734 --> 00:01:02.710
Because the buildings are perfectly level and two-dimensional,

00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:07.053
the&nbsp;skyline itself is piecewise rectangular described&nbsp;by its corners.

00:01:07.053 --> 00:01:08.664
Since the rectangles are perfectly&nbsp;level,

00:01:08.664 --> 00:01:10.960
we don't need all the corners to describe the skyline.

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:14.713
Since whenever there is a change&nbsp;in height, the x-coordinate doesn't change.

00:01:14.713 --> 00:01:19.299
That means, we can fully specify the skyline using just&nbsp;the points where the height changed

00:01:19.299 --> 00:01:22.494
which is what the problem statement for this problem expects.

00:01:22.494 --> 00:01:26.337
So, we take in the buildings as a list of left, right, height coordinates

00:01:26.337 --> 00:01:30.389
And we output the skyline&nbsp;defined by those points where the height changes

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:34.941
Okay, let's get to the code.

00:01:34.941 --> 00:01:39.080
The first thing I want&nbsp;to point out is this horrible decision that leetcode has made.

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:44.259
According to leetcode, buildings and&nbsp;points are represented by standard vectors of ints.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.246
Even though both of these things have&nbsp;fixed sizes

00:01:49.246 --> 00:01:54.194
where structs like these or at the very least arrays would be the&nbsp;fastest choice

00:01:54.194 --> 00:01:56.317
we're forced to use a vector.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.301
I'll use these using declarations for now.

00:02:00.301 --> 00:02:06.170
But at&nbsp;the end, I'll show you how we can go way faster if we just use fixed size types.

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:09.737
I'll start off&nbsp;defining these getters for the building and point attributes.

00:02:09.737 --> 00:02:11.801
Don't risk using the wrong index by&nbsp;accident.

00:02:11.801 --> 00:02:14.557
These are all going to be optimized away anyway.

00:02:14.557 --> 00:02:16.589
Again, if buildings and points were just&nbsp;structs,

00:02:16.589 --> 00:02:18.941
we wouldn't need any of these functions.

00:02:18.941 --> 00:02:21.789
Okay, let's get to computing the actual skyline.

00:02:21.789 --> 00:02:25.319
First, let's make a vector of points to hold the skyline to return.

00:02:25.319 --> 00:02:28.748
We're going to reserve two times&nbsp;as many points as there are buildings

00:02:28.748 --> 00:02:33.068
because a building can contribute at most two points to the&nbsp;skyline.

00:02:33.068 --> 00:02:36.377
The algorithm I'm going to describe is a single pass algorithm

00:02:36.377 --> 00:02:39.375
which means that we're just&nbsp;going to look at each building once.

00:02:39.375 --> 00:02:43.178
And by the end of it, we'll have the entire skyline.

00:02:43.178 --> 00:02:45.247
The idea is as&nbsp;follows.

00:02:45.247 --> 00:02:47.520
We loop through the buildings one by one.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:48.854
When we read in a building,

00:02:48.854 --> 00:02:53.060
the goal of that&nbsp;iteration is to compute all the points of the&nbsp;skyline up to

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:57.994
and including the left endpoint&nbsp;of the current building.

00:02:57.994 --> 00:02:59.550
As you can see in the animation,

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:02.368
sometimes that means adding a point&nbsp;at the current building.

00:03:02.368 --> 00:03:06.325
Sometimes it means filling in points from right edges of previous&nbsp;buildings.

00:03:06.325 --> 00:03:08.877
And sometimes no new points are added.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:19.774
First, let's focus on how do we tell if we need&nbsp;to add a point for the current building's left&nbsp;endpoint.

00:03:19.774 --> 00:03:25.200
Well, we only add a new point to the&nbsp;skyline if the tallest building so far changes.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:30.560
So, in particular, we need to be keeping&nbsp;track of what's the tallest building so far.&nbsp;

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:34.187
We'll use a priority queue that&nbsp;compares buildings by their heights

00:03:34.187 --> 00:03:40.720
in order to keep track of which building is&nbsp;the tallest that we haven't accounted for yet.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:43.879
Here's how you define a custom compare function.

00:03:43.879 --> 00:03:47.360
And here's the priority queue that we're using.

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:51.440
We're just using a standard vector as&nbsp;the underlying container for the queue.

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:55.920
We're also doing a little trick here in&nbsp;order to reserve space for the priority queue.

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:00.640
We create the vector that we want to use as&nbsp;the underlying storage for the priority queue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:03.549
and reserve the number of elements that we&nbsp;want.

00:04:03.549 --> 00:04:07.091
In this case, we reserve enough space for the total number of buildings.

00:04:07.091 --> 00:04:10.160
Since, at&nbsp;most, all of the buildings can be active.

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:14.720
One of the constructors of the priority&nbsp;queue then allows you to steal that data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:18.640
We chose a priority queue because we want&nbsp;constant time lookup of the tallest thing.

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:22.901
And it only costs us logn time to insert&nbsp;and remove.

00:04:22.901 --> 00:04:24.293
Okay, back to the loop.

00:04:24.293 --> 00:04:29.007
Whenever we see a new building, we go ahead and push&nbsp;it on to our active list.

00:04:29.007 --> 00:04:36.320
Then we go ahead and try to update the skyline with the current&nbsp;x value and whatever the new tallest height is.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:38.266
Getting the maximum height is pretty&nbsp;straightforward.

00:04:38.266 --> 00:04:42.411
Just look at the top thing on the priority queue or 0 if the queue is empty.

00:04:42.411 --> 00:04:46.346
Here's&nbsp;where we're using that the skyline is zero in the absence of any buildings.

00:04:46.346 --> 00:04:51.436
Updating the skyline just&nbsp;involves pushing the x, y coordinate onto the back of the vector.

00:04:51.436 --> 00:04:54.562
Of course, we only add a point&nbsp;to the skyline if it changes the height.

00:04:54.562 --> 00:04:59.148
So, if we try to push something that has the same height&nbsp;as whatever is on the back of the skyline already,

00:04:59.148 --> 00:05:00.749
we just do nothing.

00:05:00.749 --> 00:05:05.400
If all the buildings had&nbsp;unique left endpoints, then this would actually be fine.

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:10.339
But it is possible that the two buildings&nbsp;both have the same left endpoint.

00:05:10.339 --> 00:05:12.698
If multiple buildings all have the same left endpoint,

00:05:12.698 --> 00:05:16.080
I'm&nbsp;still going to add just one point to the skyline.

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:22.103
For that reason, I just push all of the buildings&nbsp;that have the same left endpoint in the same&nbsp;iteration of the loop.

00:05:22.103 --> 00:05:27.288
So, this works for finding&nbsp;the skyline points that are left endpoints at the beginning of buildings.

00:05:27.288 --> 00:05:31.280
But what about ones that&nbsp;are defined by the right endpoints of buildings?

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:34.015
When we go to the next iteration of the loop,

00:05:34.015 --> 00:05:37.840
it's possible that we missed many right endpoints.

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:46.138
Whenever our current x exceeds the right endpoint of the tallest building,

00:05:46.138 --> 00:05:49.554
that means that the tallest building is about to drop off.

00:05:49.554 --> 00:05:53.097
And that's&nbsp;a candidate for when we need to add a new skyline point.

00:05:53.097 --> 00:05:55.048
The logic for this one is a little bit&nbsp;complicated.

00:05:55.048 --> 00:05:57.349
So, let's put it in its own function.

00:05:57.349 --> 00:05:59.256
So, here's the function.

00:05:59.256 --> 00:06:05.370
The purpose of this&nbsp;function is to handle the case when the tallest active building is about to drop off.

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:06.581
I know it's&nbsp;just a few lines.

00:06:06.581 --> 00:06:11.040
But there's a lot of thought that goes into making sure that they do the correct&nbsp;thing.

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:12.125
The idea is simple.

00:06:12.125 --> 00:06:17.793
Just pop off whatever the tallest thing is and then try to add a new&nbsp;skyline point at whatever the new tallest thing is.

00:06:17.793 --> 00:06:20.913
The subtle issue here is that, for speed,

00:06:20.913 --> 00:06:23.855
we don't want to go through the entire priority queue

00:06:23.855 --> 00:06:29.280
and get rid of all of&nbsp;the things that should be deactivated.

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:32.218
That means that whenever we pop something off the&nbsp;queue,

00:06:32.218 --> 00:06:34.949
the next tallest thing might be something

00:06:34.949 --> 00:06:38.483
that should have been removed a long time ago.

00:06:38.483 --> 00:06:40.302
We keep popping things off the top

00:06:40.302 --> 00:06:44.082
until we see something whose right endpoint is strictly further

00:06:44.082 --> 00:06:47.033
than the tallest building that we started with.

00:06:47.033 --> 00:06:52.823
Any tallest building whose right endpoint is left&nbsp;of our original tallest building's right endpoint,

00:06:52.823 --> 00:06:55.924
cannot be part of the skyline.

00:06:55.924 --> 00:06:59.925
It was either a&nbsp;shorter building that had the same right endpoint

00:06:59.925 --> 00:07:04.333
or it was something that should have&nbsp;been removed from a previous iteration

00:07:04.333 --> 00:07:09.200
that we didn't remove in order to avoid&nbsp;traversing the entire priority queue.

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:13.920
That's why we're removing a bunch of&nbsp;things but only update the skyline once.

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:16.852
That also explains the name of the function.

00:07:16.852 --> 00:07:18.874
We keep popping off the tallest thing

00:07:18.874 --> 00:07:23.804
until the right endpoint that we find is&nbsp;past the current tallest right endpoint.

00:07:23.804 --> 00:07:25.708
Returning back to the main loop.

00:07:25.708 --> 00:07:28.819
We keep popping&nbsp;off the tallest building and stale buildings

00:07:28.819 --> 00:07:32.113
updating the skyline until we find that the&nbsp;tallest thing remaining

00:07:32.113 --> 00:07:36.133
has a right endpoint further than our current x

00:07:36.133 --> 00:07:38.164
or&nbsp;until nothing active is left.

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:43.760
This takes care of everything up until the end.

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:47.809
But there's a little bit more work to do after we&nbsp;get to the end of the buildings.

00:07:47.809 --> 00:07:50.808
We basically just pretend that there's a building at infinity.

00:07:50.808 --> 00:07:53.970
And&nbsp;then pop off all of the remaining active buildings.

00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:02.755
Then of course don't forget to return the skyline.

00:08:02.755 --> 00:08:03.770
I wrote a bunch of tests.

00:08:03.770 --> 00:08:06.185
And I'm timing both how long it takes in total.

00:08:06.185 --> 00:08:10.720
And how long it takes&nbsp;just to run the actual get skyline function.

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:11.874
Running it a bunch of times,

00:08:11.874 --> 00:08:17.102
we see that it passes&nbsp;all of my tests and does decent in terms of timing.

00:08:17.102 --> 00:08:19.998
But I think we can do better than&nbsp;that.

00:08:19.998 --> 00:08:23.897
I looked back at the code and once we put something into the active queue,

00:08:23.897 --> 00:08:27.923
we&nbsp;never actually use anything except for its height and its right endpoint.

00:08:27.923 --> 00:08:30.149
We never use the left&nbsp;endpoint.

00:08:30.149 --> 00:08:34.839
Additionally, since this active queue is something that's internal to our algorithm,

00:08:34.839 --> 00:08:38.160
we can choose a better data type than a vector.

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:42.640
Here, we define a fixed size struct that's&nbsp;just the right and the height of the building.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:48.709
And we give it an explicit constructor to convert&nbsp;a building into just the right and height.

00:08:48.709 --> 00:08:51.977
Then we change our compare function and our priority&nbsp;queue

00:08:51.977 --> 00:08:55.889
to take in these fixed size structures instead of vectors.

00:08:55.889 --> 00:08:59.467
This allows us to get rid of&nbsp;two of our getters.

00:08:59.467 --> 00:09:02.445
Then everywhere that we used getRight or getHeight,

00:09:02.445 --> 00:09:05.398
we change to dot write&nbsp;or dot height.

00:09:05.398 --> 00:09:08.130
Also, instead of pushing building objects onto our active queue,

00:09:08.130 --> 00:09:13.120
we're now pushing&nbsp;these fixed size structs onto the active queue.

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:16.854
Making this change took the total test&nbsp;time about 5 milliseconds down

00:09:16.854 --> 00:09:22.880
both in the total test time and time&nbsp;spent in the getSkyline function.

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:25.129
Okay, remember the comment that I made at&nbsp;the beginning

00:09:25.129 --> 00:09:28.581
where I said that using a&nbsp;vector events here was a mistake?

00:09:28.581 --> 00:09:33.280
Watch what&nbsp;happens just by switching to fixed size arrays.

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:40.480
Making no other changes, our times are now&nbsp;down to 6 milliseconds and 1 millisecond.

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:48.364
We went from 40 and 15 down to about 6 and 1 doing&nbsp;nothing but changing to fixed size arrays.

00:09:48.364 --> 00:09:52.221
And this I think is one of the biggest problems with&nbsp;leetcode.

00:09:52.221 --> 00:09:57.340
This was supposed to be a competition for how fast you can make this algorithm go.

00:09:57.340 --> 00:10:01.893
But&nbsp;by telling you that a building and a point are supposed to be vectors of ints,

00:10:01.893 --> 00:10:06.240
they've already&nbsp;slowed your program down by a factor of 5.

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:09.005
This just goes to show that designing a good&nbsp;interface

00:10:09.005 --> 00:10:11.861
and choosing your problem statement&nbsp;as carefully

00:10:11.861 --> 00:10:15.600
and as correctly as&nbsp;possible can really make a difference.

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:20.640
If you do something, like, choose an inappropriate&nbsp;type for your function or return type,

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:23.819
you can end up really slowing down the execution&nbsp;of your function.

00:10:23.819 --> 00:10:25.966
and then you also end up slowing down other things

00:10:25.966 --> 00:10:32.811
that now have to pass&nbsp;data around, reformat data into vectors in order to call the function.

00:10:32.811 --> 00:10:35.693
Anyway, I hope you&nbsp;enjoyed this solution.

00:10:35.693 --> 00:10:38.179
If you did, don't forget to subscribe.

00:10:38.179 --> 00:10:42.880
As always, thank you to my patrons&nbsp;and donors. I really appreciate your support.

00:10:42.880 --> 00:10:45.994
If you especially like the video, please consider&nbsp;becoming a patron.

00:10:45.994 --> 00:10:48.320
Thanks and see you next time.

